WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
You've seen how using momentum can speed up gradient descent.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.440
There's another algorithm called RMS prop, which stands for Root Mean Square Prop.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.560
They can also speed up gradient descent.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.760
Let's see how it works.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.560
Recall our example from before that if you implement gradient descent, you can end up

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.600
with huge oscillations in the vertical direction even while it's trying to make progress in

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.680
the horizontal direction.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.720
In order to provide intuition for this example, let's say that the vertical axis is the

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.040
parameter B and the horizontal axis is the parameter W.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.840
It really could be W1 and W2 or some of the same parameters.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.560
Notice it's named as B and W for the sake of intuition.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.720
You want to slow down the learning in the B direction or in the vertical direction and

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:55.080
speed up learning or at least not slow it down in the horizontal direction.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.560
This is what the RMS prop algorithm does to accomplish this.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:16.060
On iteration T, it will compute as usual the derivative dW dB on the current mini batch.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.700
It's going to keep this exponentially weighted average.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.940
Instead of VdW, I'm going to use new notation SdW.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:33.940
SdW is equal to beta times their previous value plus 1 minus beta times dW squared.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.540
I sometimes write this dW star star 2 to denoted the exponentiation.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.300
I'm going to write this as dW squared.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:48.820
For clarity, this squaring operation is an element-wise squaring operation.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:56.220
What this is doing is really keeping an exponentially weighted average of the squares of the derivatives.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:04.260
And similarly, we also have SdB equals beta SdB plus 1 minus beta dB squared.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.340
And again, the squaring is an element-wise operation.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.500
Next, RMS prop then updates the parameters as follows.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.660
W gets updated as W minus the learning rate.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:22.100
And whereas previously we had alpha times dW.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:28.260
Now it's dW divided by square root of SdW.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:35.100
And B gets updated as B minus the learning rate times instead of just the gradient.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.660
This is also divided by SdB.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.940
So let's gain some intuition about how this works.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:48.820
Recall that in the horizontal direction, or in this example in the W direction, we want

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.500
learning to go pretty fast.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:55.100
Whereas in the vertical direction, or in this example in the B direction, we want to slow

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.280
down or to damp out the oscillations in the vertical direction.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:08.500
So with these terms, SdW and SdB, what we're hoping is that SdW will be relatively small.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.140
So that here we're dividing by a relatively small number.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.820
Whereas SdB would be relatively large.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.660
So that here we're dividing by a relatively large number in order to slow down the updates

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.460
in the vertical direction.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:27.040
And indeed if you look at the derivatives, these derivatives are much larger in the vertical

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.360
direction than in the horizontal direction.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.780
So the slope is very large in the B direction.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:40.940
So with derivatives like this, this is a very large dB and a relatively small dW.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:45.260
Because the function is sloped much more steeply in the vertical direction than is in

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.900
the B direction than in the W direction, than in the horizontal direction.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.380
And so dB squared would be relatively large, so SdB would be relatively large.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.420
Whereas compared to that, dW would be smaller, or dW squared would be smaller, and so SdW

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.100
would be smaller.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:07.420
So the net effect of this is that your updates in the vertical direction are divided by a

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.260
much larger number, and so that helps damp out the oscillations.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:15.420
Whereas the updates in the horizontal direction are divided by a smaller number.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.180
So the net impact of using RMS prop is that your updates will end up looking more like

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.180
this.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:30.340
Right, that your updates in the vertical direction get damped out, but in the horizontal

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.420
direction it can keep going.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.740
And one effect of this is also that you could therefore use a larger learning rate

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:41.500
alpha and get faster learning without it diverging in the vertical direction.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.700
Now just for the sake of clarity, I've been calling the vertical and horizontal

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:48.500
directions B and W, just to illustrate this.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:53.340
In practice, you're in a very high dimensional space of parameters, so maybe

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.900
the vertical dimensions where you're trying to damp out oscillations is some set of

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:07.380
parameters, W1, W2, W17, and the horizontal dimensions might be W3, W4, and so on.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:11.100
And so the separation is W and B is just for illustration.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:16.580
In practice, dW is a very high dimensional parameter vector, dB is also a very high

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.980
dimensional parameter vector, but the intuition is that in dimensions where you're

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:26.900
getting these oscillations, you end up computing a larger sum of weighted

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.380
average for these squares of derivatives, and so you end up damping out the

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.100
directions in which there are these oscillations.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:38.220
So that's RMS prop, and it stands for root mean squared because root mean

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.140
squared prop, because here you're squaring the derivatives and then you take the

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.100
square root here at the end.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.860
So finally, just a couple last details on this algorithm before we move on.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.060
In the next video, we're actually going to combine RMS prop together with

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.380
momentum.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:00.340
So rather than using the hyperparameter beta, which we had used for momentum,

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:05.540
I'm going to call this hyperparameter beta 2, just to not clash, you know, the

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:09.220
same hyperparameter for both momentum and for RMS prop.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:14.380
And also to make sure that your algorithm doesn't divide by zero, you know, what if

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:19.620
square root of SDW, right, is very close to zero, then this thing could blow up

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.380
just to ensure numerical stability.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.660
When you implement this in practice, you add a very, very small epsilon to the

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:27.660
denominator.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.500
It doesn't really matter what epsilon is.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.340
You use 10 to the negative 8 would be a reasonable default, but this just

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:38.500
ensures slightly greater numerical stability that, you know, for numerical

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:42.060
round off, for the reason that you don't end up dividing by a very small

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:43.060
number.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:48.340
So that's RMS prop, and similar to momentum has the effect of damping out

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.900
the oscillations in gradient descent, in meanly-batch gradient descent, and

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:57.100
allowing you to maybe use a larger learning rate alpha, and certainly

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.900
speeding up the learning speed of your algorithm.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.220
So now you know how to implement RMS prop, and this will be another way for

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.900
you to speed up your learning algorithm.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:11.460
One fun fact about RMS prop, it was actually first proposed, not in an

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.620
academic research paper, but in a Coursera course that Jeff Hinton had

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.620
taught on Coursera many years ago.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:22.540
I guess Coursera wasn't intended to be a platform for dissemination of

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.140
novel academic research, but it worked out pretty well in that case.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:29.620
It was really from the Coursera course that RMS prop started to become

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.620
widely known, and it really took off.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.300
We've talked about momentum, we've talked about RMS prop.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.180
It turns out that if you put them together, you can get an even

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.540
better optimization algorithm.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.020
Let's talk about that in the next video.
